// mn-resume-world — STEP 5 gate: TCont carries the effect-WORLD; the
// world-index is INERT on the OneShot path (Law 7) and the unify arm that
// now compares disciplines AND unifies world-rows never spuriously fires
// E_ResumeWorldMismatch on a well-typed single-world program.
//
// A OneShot handler arm (one resume site, not under a loop → OneShot) whose
// continuation type is TCont(Int, OneShot, <world>). The world is the ambient
// effect-row at the suspend point (here: the Ask effect itself). The arm
// resumes once with the configured answer; the body adds 1. Compiling this
// drives the continuation type through unify (the keystone arm) with a SINGLE
// consistent world, so disciplines_compatible holds and unify_row succeeds —
// no E_ResumeWorldMismatch. The OneShot lowering stays the byte-identical
// LReturn floor (every ev*/eq micro proves this is unperturbed).
//
// The CROSS-WORLD value gate — two continuations frozen under DIFFERENT
// handler-sets meeting at a persist/resume boundary, surfacing
// E_ResumeWorldMismatch — the VALUE gate (the runtime/load-time twin of this
// compile-time unify check) is now REALIZED: the continuation record carries
// the world_tag field (W06) and the Persist handler (lib/runtime/persist.mn,
// W08) memcpy's it through the persist/rehydrate boundary, comparing the
// captured world against the live world on load (tests/micros/mn-persist.mn is
// its round-trip gate). The peer Hβ.types.resume-world-mismatch-value-gate is
// thus DISCHARGED at the .mn layer; the seed catches up to the Persist chain
// (Hβ.seed.durable-persist). Law 7 — this OneShot micro stays byte-identical.
//
// Run: tools/run-micro.sh tests/micros/mn-resume-world.mn 42
// Expected exit 42: resume(41) flows to body, body adds 1.

effect Ask { ask() -> Int }

handler give with answer = 41 {
  ask() => {
    let r = resume(answer)
    r
  }
}

fn body() = ask() + 1

fn main(q) = { body() } ~> give
